<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siamun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siamun was a king of the Twenty-first Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a> for about nineteen years in the 10th century BCE. He enlarged the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> at Tanis and built at Memphis. A relief showing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> smiting a foe, apparently holding a Philistine axe, suggests a campaign in Philistia, and some scholars identify him with the pharaoh who in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> captured Gezer and gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siamun.jpg" alt="Siamun" width="272" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W. M. Flinders Petrie (1853-1942) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Relief_Siamun_Petrie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Siamun, whose throne name was Netjerkheperre-Setepenamun, was the sixth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the Twenty-first Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. He was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psusennes-i">Psusennes I</a> and may have been a child when his father died, which could have led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osorkon-the-elder">Osorkon the Elder</a> to act as king-regent. A queen Karimala, known from an inscription in the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semna">Semna</a>, may have been his wife and the daughter of Osorkon the Elder; Chris Bennett argued in 1999 for this marriage. Siamun was not succeeded by a son but by a cousin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psusennes-ii">Psusennes II</a>, which suggests a crisis over the succession.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The highest attested year of Siamun is year 17, recorded in the Karnak Priestly Annals at the induction of Hori, son of Nespaneferhor, into the priesthood. Rolf Krauss calculated from a lunar feast that this year equals 970 BCE, which would put his accession in 986 BCE. The inscription is the first in which the word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> is used as a title attached to a king's royal name; before then it had referred only to the office of the king.</p><p>According to Nicolas Grimal, Siamun doubled the size of the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a>, worked at the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> at Mesen, and built at Heliopolis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses">Piramesse</a>. At Memphis he dedicated a new temple to Amun and favoured the priests of Ptah.</p><p>A fragmentary relief from the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a> shows a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> named Siamun smiting enemies and holding a double-bladed axe. Kenneth Kitchen argued that its shape is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> rather than Canaanite, so that Siamun's foes were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Philistines</a> and the scene commemorated a recent victory at Gezer. Paul S. Ash replied that the relief shows a fictitious battle, that Philistines are never shown with axes and that nothing in it points to Philistia or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>.</p><p>Excavations at Gezer show Egyptian destruction of the city in the 10th century BCE, in Siamun's time. Some scholars, among them Kitchen and William G. Dever, identify him with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> who gave his daughter in marriage to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and took Gezer for him. Paul S. Ash and Mark W. Chavalas challenge this, and Edward Lipinski argues that Siamun died before Solomon's accession and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Shoshenq I</a> was more likely the conqueror of Gezer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Siamun's original tomb has never been found. Ushabtis bearing his name were found on two decayed mummies in the antechamber of the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psusennes-i">Psusennes I</a>, and it has been proposed that he was reburied there after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> flooded his own tomb.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siamun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The intact royal tombs found there by Pierre Montet in 1939 to 1940 include the silver coffin and gold mask of Psusennes I. <b>Siamun</b> probably campaigned in Philistia, and Osorkon the Elder was a Libyan.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-first-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-first Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
